-- title:
--   Bialgebras generated by Witt coordinates have local Cartier dual
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ be a field, $p$ a prime with $\operatorname{char} k = p$, and $n$ a natural number. Let $A$ be a commutative ring carrying a $k$-bialgebra structure whose comultiplication is cocommutative, and assume $A$ is finite as a $k$-module. Let $\mathrm{wittHom}\,k\,p\,n\,A$ denote the additive subgroup of the truncated Witt vectors $W_n(A)$ consisting of those $x$ with $W_n(\Delta)(x) = W_n(\iota_1)(x) + W_n(\iota_2)(x)$ in $W_n(A \otimes_k A)$, where $\Delta$ is the comultiplication of $A$ viewed as a ring map and $\iota_1, \iota_2 : A \to A \otimes_k A$ are the two inclusions; these are the additive, i.e. group-scheme, homomorphisms from $\operatorname{Spec} A$ to $W_n$. The hypothesis is that the $k$-subalgebra of $A$ generated by the set of all coefficients $x_i$, for $x \in \mathrm{wittHom}\,k\,p\,n\,A$ and $i \in \{0,\dots,n-1\}$, is all of $A$. The conclusion is twofold: first, every $\beta$ in the convolution ring on $A \to_{\ell} k$ whose underlying linear form kills $1 \in A$ satisfies $\beta^{p^n} = 0$, the power being taken for the convolution product; second, the Cartier dual $\operatorname{Hom}_k(A,k)$, with its convolution ring structure, is a local ring.
--
--   This is the statement that a finite commutative group scheme which is cut out by the coefficients of its homomorphisms to the truncated Witt group $W_n$ — equivalently, which embeds as a closed subgroup of a power of $W_n$ — is unipotent: the augmentation ideal of its Cartier dual is killed by the $p^n$-th convolution power, so that dual is local. It feeds the construction of Dieudonné data and of Hopf algebras over $\mathbb{Z}/p$ attached to nilpotent situations, and the analysis of kernels of maps to Witt groups.
